1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,120
We are now in the magnificent hall of mirrors, one of the most evocative places on the

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:09,200
entire tourist route through the Grata del Bue Marino.

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:14,960
In this section, the water is usually so still and flat that it becomes a perfect reflecting

4
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:16,200
surface.

5
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:22,160
The walls and formations mirror themselves, creating an upside-down image of extraordinary

6
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:27,680
clarity, giving the illusion of a depth far greater than it actually is.

7
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:33,680
It is, however, only an optical illusion, at the deepest point accessible to the public,

8
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:37,440
the water never exceeds two meters.

9
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:43,360
The formation of these mirrors depends greatly on external weather conditions.

10
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:50,240
When a dry wind blows outside, such as the maestroly, the air remains stable and the surface

11
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:55,600
of the water stays perfectly still, allowing the reflections to appear in all their

12
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:56,600
perfection.

13
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:02,960
If, on the other hand, a humid wind is blowing outside, such as the Ceraco, the moisture

14
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:05,320
penetrates into the cave.

15
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:10,760
Here it meets a naturally cooler environment and a condensation effect occurs.

16
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:17,760
The warm, humid air, upon contact with the cooler walls, produces tiny water droplets.

17
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:23,960
In these cases, inside the hall of mirrors, a kind of very fine drizzle can begin, which

18
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:29,080
ripples the surface of the water and makes the mirror affect disappear.

19
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:34,280
The temperature inside the cave is in fact constant throughout the year, ranging between

20
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:37,400
16 and 18 degrees Celsius.

21
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:42,400
The relative humidity is very high and can reach 99%.

22
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:48,480
It is precisely this delicate balance between temperature, humidity, and external conditions

23
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:53,520
that determines the magic or the absence of the mirrors.

24
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:58,600
This hall is also one of the best places to observe the colors of the cave.

25
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:04,040
White is the natural color of the limestone, the sedimentary rock of which the entire

26
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:06,000
mountain is composed.

27
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,440
The red orange is due to the presence of iron oxide, a common element in carst environments.

28
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:15,920
The brown tones are linked to detrital deposits.

29
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:21,000
Mud, soil, and materials transported by water over time.

30
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,640
Also visible on the walls is a thin horizontal brown line, which runs from the hall of

31
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:31,400
Candelabras to approximately the halfway point of the route.

32
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:37,360
On closer inspection, it turns out to be composed of oyster fossils dating back approximately

33
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:42,520
120,000 to 125,000 years.

34
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:48,960
This line is evidence of an ancient sea level, probably following a glacial period.

35
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:54,480
With the melting of the ice, the sea rose to a height greater than today, allowing oysters

36
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:57,480
to settle along the cave walls.

37
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:03,080
With the subsequent lowering of the sea level, these organisms became fossilized, leaving

38
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:06,840
a trace that is still perfectly visible today.

39
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:12,200
The same line marking that ancient sea level can also be observed along the coasts of the

40
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:18,640
Gulf of Orsay, where it appears as a clear wave-cut notch carved into the rock.

41
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:23,720
Here in the hall of mirrors, we are not merely observing reflections.

42
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:28,960
We are observing time, climate, and geological history imprinted in stone.

